  ///1DIR

  The DIR  command  displays   the   names   of   files   and   the
  characteristics associated with the files.

  The DIR command has three distinct references:

          DIR
          DIRS
          DIR with Options

  DIR and DI      134
                      150      300        600        1200
                      1800     2400       3600       4800
                      7200     9600       19200

  ///2Examples

  A>DEVICE

       Displays the physical devices and   c that
       drive,  and from any drive in the search chain (see SETDEF).

  ///2withOptions

  Syntax:

          DIR {d:} [options]
          DIR {filespec} {filespec} ... [options]

  Explanation:

  The DIR command with options is an enhanc the 
	  prompts as follows:
	        Replace Which Drive ? (A-P) or (Q)uit: A
	        A with Which Drive? (A-P)  or  (Q)uit: C
		NOTE: A->C or C->A work equally.
		Only A through Q, ?, and cntl C are valid responses.
		Q in the first prompt will wrRS are built-in  utilities.   DIR  with  Options  is  a
  transient utility and must be loaded into memory from the disk.

  ///2Built-in

  Syntax:

          DIR  {d:}
          DIR  {filespec}

          DIRS {d:}
          DIRS {filespec}
urrent  assignments  of
       the logical devices in the system.

  A>DEVICE NAMES

       Lists the physical devices with a   summary  of  the  device
       characteristics.

  A>DEVICE VALUES

       Displays  the  current  logical  device ased version of   the  DIR
  built-in  command  and  displays your files in a variety of ways.
  DIR can search for files on any or all  drives, for  any  or  all
  user  numbers.  One  or  two letters is sufficient to identify an
